EXPERIMENT 18: EVEN OR ODD NUMBER USING JUNIT ASSERTIONS

## 1. AIM

To write a Java program to check whether a given number is even or odd and validate the output using JUnit assertions.

## 2. ALGORITHM

1. Start the program.
2. Read an integer number.
3. Divide the number by 2.
4. Check the remainder using the modulus (%) operator.
5. If the remainder is 0, the number is even.
6. Otherwise, the number is odd.
7. Use JUnit assertions to validate the result.
8. Display the output and stop the program.

## 3. CODE

### File 1: EvenOdd.java

```java
public class EvenOdd {

    public static boolean isEven(int number) {
        return number % 2 == 0;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int number = 10;

        if (isEven(number)) {
            System.out.println(number + " is Even");
        } else {
            System.out.println(number + " is Odd");
        }
    }
}
```

### File 2: EvenOddTest.java

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class EvenOddTest {

    @Test
    public void testEvenNumber() {
        assertTrue(EvenOdd.isEven(10));
    }

    @Test
    public void testOddNumber() {
        assertFalse(EvenOdd.isEven(7));
    }

    @Test
    public void testZero() {
        assertTrue(EvenOdd.isEven(0));
    }

    @Test
    public void testNegativeNumber() {
        assertTrue(EvenOdd.isEven(-8));
    }
}
```

